1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,830
How do you build a telescope the size of a continent?

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:08,670
Put one antenna in Sardinia and another thousands of kilometres away.

3
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:10,959
Point them at the same galaxy.

4
00:00:11,009 --> 00:00:13,490
Then bring their signals together.

5
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:19,858
A computer in the Netherlands can combine those measurements while the telescopes are still observing.

6
00:00:19,908 --> 00:00:23,390
Details that either antenna would miss come into focus.

7
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:28,490
To see what makes that possible, follow the signal.

8
00:00:28,740 --> 00:00:32,519
The first links on this map grew out of a practical problem.

9
00:00:32,569 --> 00:00:35,503
Universities were building national networks,

10
00:00:35,553 --> 00:00:39,050
but their research partners were often across a border.

11
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:44,555
In 1993, DANTE began coordinating those connections.

12
00:00:44,605 --> 00:00:49,595
In 1997, TEN-34 linked researchers across Europe.

13
00:00:49,645 --> 00:00:53,220
In 2001, GÉANT went into service.

14
00:00:53,470 --> 00:01:00,246
The European Union co-funds this work alongside the national research and education networks.

15
00:01:00,296 --> 00:01:02,815
Today, through Horizon Europe,

16
00:01:02,865 --> 00:01:08,830
the EU helps those partners expand and maintain infrastructure they use together.

17
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:11,618
Start at a university in Sofia.

18
00:01:11,668 --> 00:01:15,002
Its national network is the way into GÉANT.

19
00:01:15,052 --> 00:01:21,970
Across Europe, this arrangement serves around ten thousand institutions and fifty million people.

20
00:01:22,220 --> 00:01:29,890
In January 2026, GÉANT completed a backbone upgrade supporting links of eight hundred gigabits per second.

21
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:31,719
What does that buy?

22
00:01:31,769 --> 00:01:32,870
Capacity.

23
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:43,300
A telescope can keep streaming its observations while satellite images and data from physics experiments travel over the same infrastructure.

24
00:01:43,550 --> 00:01:48,963
Near Geneva, two protons collide inside CERN’s Large Hadron Collider.

25
00:01:49,013 --> 00:01:52,510
A detector records the tracks left behind.

26
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:55,985
Could one of them be evidence of a new particle?

27
00:01:56,035 --> 00:01:58,910
One collision cannot answer that question.

28
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:06,029
Research networks carry the data to a worldwide grid of more than a hundred and seventy computing sites.

29
00:02:06,079 --> 00:02:10,600
Physicists compare vast numbers of events until a pattern emerges.

30
00:02:10,850 --> 00:02:18,370
The analysis behind the discovery of the Higgs boson in 2012 ran on that shared grid.

31
00:02:18,620 --> 00:02:21,477
Follow the next line under the Atlantic.

32
00:02:21,527 --> 00:02:25,534
Through BELLA, a programme co-funded by the European Union,

33
00:02:25,584 --> 00:02:32,620
a submarine cable between Portugal and Brazil connects GÉANT with Latin America’s research network, RedCLARA.

34
00:02:32,870 --> 00:02:36,027
For access to Copernicus Earth observation data,

35
00:02:36,077 --> 00:02:40,380
the European Commission reported a drop from sixty minutes to seven.

36
00:02:40,630 --> 00:02:43,712
What can a business do with satellite data?

37
00:02:43,762 --> 00:02:50,960
In Mauritania, a startup developed a service that helps rice farmers identify fields that may need irrigation.

38
00:02:51,210 --> 00:02:56,920
A satellite image becomes a decision about where to send water.

39
00:02:57,170 --> 00:03:00,160
For a student visiting another university,

40
00:03:00,210 --> 00:03:04,903
the first sign of this cooperation may be a familiar Wi-Fi connection.

41
00:03:04,953 --> 00:03:09,950
With eduroam, her laptop can connect using her home university credentials.

42
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:17,290
Through eduGAIN, that same university identity can give her access to participating research services.

43
00:03:17,540 --> 00:03:21,780
And when her team needs cloud storage or computing power,

44
00:03:21,830 --> 00:03:26,400
the university can purchase it through GÉANT’s OCRE framework.

45
00:03:26,650 --> 00:03:30,590
On a map, a research network looks like a set of lines.

46
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:34,536
Follow one, and a distant galaxy comes into focus.

47
00:03:34,586 --> 00:03:38,670
Follow another, and a farmer knows which field to check.

48
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:42,250
That is what those lines carry.
